                            Brute Force
class Solution {
    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
        int n = heights.length;
        int maxArea = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int minHeight = heights[i];

            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                minHeight = Math.min(minHeight, heights[j]);
                int width = j - i + 1;
                int area = minHeight * width;
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, area);
            }
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
}


              Intuition
The simplest approach is to consider every possible rectangle in the histogram.
Treat each index as the starting point of a rectangle.
Extend the rectangle one bar at a time towards the right.
While extending, continuously maintain the minimum height among all bars included in the current rectangle.
Since the rectangle's height is limited by the shortest bar, calculate:
Width = number of bars included
Area = minimum height × width
Compare the calculated area with the maximum area obtained so far.
By checking all possible subarrays of the histogram, the algorithm guarantees finding the largest rectangle, but it performs many repeated calculations.

            Time Complexity
O(n)
